# 0071-built-game-stays-when-its-file-is-deleted.patch -- a game the app built stays in the library when its game file (.nsp .xci .nsz .nup) is deleted by hand in the Files app: an empty placeholder of the same
#   name is put back and marked "removed", so it is a 0068 stub ("File removed", name, cover, settings, saves, mods, Play and Export IPA when it is the build on disk; "Add it again" for everything that needs the content).
# Applies after 0069 (the tree with 0010..0069 applied; 0070 is written in parallel and touches none of the hunks below, but GameAppMain.cs hunks may then apply with an offset). UNTESTED ON DEVICE: compiled
#   (dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64 -t:Compile: rc 0, 0 errors, 38 warnings: the same 38 (file and code) as the same project with the 0069 sources, none new) and the pure classes run in a host
#   harness (a net11 console program built from the real source text of GameAppSourceRemoval.cs and the files 0068's harness compiles: 55 checks, all pass; 7 mutations of the new logic, each caught; 0068's own
#   harness, built from this tree, still passes 341 of 341, as it does on the 0069 tree). No iPhone run.
#
# WHY (the owner): "If I delete the game's .nup / .xci etc. directly in the app's folder (Files app) to save space, the game disappears from the app even though it was built." The library is made from the game files
#   in Documents (Dumps()); 0068 keeps a game whose file the APP removed (a zero-byte stub + a "removed" ledger record), but a file deleted by hand left nothing.
#
# WHAT
#   * THE RECORD -- Documents/gameapp.built.tsv (BuiltRecord / BuiltLedger in GameAppSourceRemoval.cs, no UIKit): one line per base game file the app built: name, title id (GameAppUi.BaseTitleIdOf, "" when unknown),
#     size at the build, UTC time. Written like the import ledger: under one lock, through gameapp.built.tsv.part and one rename, only when it changes; an unreadable file reads as empty (no placeholder is made).
#     Only game files are recorded (never prod.keys, a path, anything else). Only the BASE game file: an update or DLC is not recorded on purpose (a placeholder of an update deleted by hand would block every rebuild
#     with "add it again" and there is no in-app way to drop an update, so it would come back forever).
#   * WRITTEN at the end of a build: RemoveSourceAfterBuild (0068's hook, called for non-boot-only runs after Finish) now calls RecordBuiltGame(game) first, when the outcome is StopOutcome.Finished and the run did not
#     fail, WHATEVER the 0068 toggle says, and only when the framework stamp's game= names this file (else `library: <game>: the build ended but the build stamp names <x>: not recorded as built`). The stamp and the
#     size are read on the main thread (one small file and one stat, before 0068's worker can replace the file by its stub); the write is on a worker.
#   * SEEDED for games built before this patch: the stamp's game= (Documents/gameapp.framework, as GameStatusOf reads it) is added when it has no record (size: the file's, else the import record's for a 0068 stub,
#     else 0), log `library: <name> recorded as built (the build on disk names it)`.
#   * KEPT (BuiltKeep.Decide / Plan / Execute; KeepBuiltGames in GameAppSourceUi.cs): at launch (RefreshGames calls it once; RefreshGames runs at launch before the first full frame) and every time the app comes back
#     to the foreground (its own WillEnterForeground observer), on a worker thread: seed, then for each record: not a game file -> nothing / the file is there (content, a 0068 stub, or an empty file put by hand:
#     each stays what 0068 says it is) -> nothing / missing but another game file in Documents has exactly the recorded size (> 0) -> taken as renamed: the record follows that name, no placeholder, log
#     `library: <name> is not in Documents but <other> has the size it had at its build: taken as the same game renamed, no placeholder` / missing -> a placeholder: the import ledger FIRST (the app's own record of
#     the file when it has one, its source kept so "Add it again from <source>" still works, marked removed now; a record already removed stays as it is; else a new record: how "deleted", source "Files", the
#     built size), then SourceStub.Replace with a rename that NEVER replaces a file (File.Move overwrite:false: a file that appeared meanwhile, an import, is left intact and the ledger is put back), log
#     `library: <name> was deleted from Documents after its build: an empty placeholder keeps it in the library (put the file back to rebuild it)`. When a placeholder was made: sizes.Invalidate + RefreshScreen.
#     A pass already running makes a second one return (Interlocked). The exported player does none of this.
#   * NEVER for a game deleted FROM THE APP: PerformDelete's delete worker removes the game's built records FIRST (before any file goes, so a kill in the middle cannot bring it back as a placeholder); if that write
#     fails the delete goes on and logs `delete: the built record could not be updated (...): the game may come back as an empty placeholder`. Never for a non-game file. A zero-byte file the player put by hand
#     with no record stays "Empty file", as 0068 treats it.
#   * 0068 SEMANTICS KEPT: the toggle off still keeps files; ShouldRemove / Plan / Execute / IsStub unchanged, except one line: Execute's "first removal ever" test ignores records with how "deleted" (a placeholder is
#     not a removal by the app), so 0068's one-time line is still said before its first real removal. ImportHow.DeletedByHand = "deleted" and SourceText.HowText("deleted") = "deleted in Files" are new.
#     A placeholder is AlreadyRemoved for 0068; the file put back BY HAND (content under a removed record) is never removed by 0068; put back by an import it gets a fresh record and 0068 may remove it again.
#
# LIMIT (by design, not fixed here): only ONE game is built at a time (one Documents/DmGameProbe.framework and one Documents/extract/RomFS.img). A placeholder keeps a game VISIBLE with its data; only the game the
#   build on disk belongs to can still be played from it. Any other placeholder game, and every game after an app update withdraws the framework, needs its file again to be rebuilt (0068's "The game file was removed"
#   alert offers to add it again).
#
# SEMANTICS TO KNOW: the placeholder is recreated each launch / return to the foreground while the game has a built record, so deleting the placeholder in Files does not remove the game; the app's Delete does.
#   0066's "Delete the build" keeps the record (the game file stays; if it is deleted by hand later the game comes back as a placeholder that needs its file to be rebuilt).
# "Remove download (stays in the cloud)" / "Delete" (RemoveFiles in GameAppLibrary.cs) drop the files' built records before deleting them, so no placeholder brings such a game back
